LeetCode 5 – Longest Palindromic Substring
Problem Description

Given a string s, return the longest palindromic substring in s.

A palindrome is a string that reads the same forward and backward.

Approach: Expand Around Center

This solution uses the Expand Around Center technique to find the longest palindromic substring efficiently.

The key observation is that every palindrome has a center. A palindrome can have:

Odd length: A single character is the center (e.g., "racecar").
Even length: Two characters form the center (e.g., "abba").

For every index, we expand outward from both possible centers and check whether the characters on the left and right are equal.

Algorithm
Initialize res as an empty string and reslen = 0 to track the longest palindrome.
Iterate through every index i in the string.
Check odd-length palindromes:
Set both pointers l and r to i.
Expand outward while the characters match.
Update the longest palindrome whenever a longer one is found.
Check even-length palindromes:
Set l = i and r = i + 1.
Expand outward while the characters match.
Update the longest palindrome whenever a longer one is found.
Return res.


Example

Input:

python
s = "babad"


Output:

python
"bab"


Explanation:

The longest palindromic substrings are "bab" and "aba", both having a length of 3.

Either answer is valid.

Complexity Analysis

Let n be the length of the input string.

Time Complexity: O(n²) – We consider two possible centers at each index and expand outward. In the worst case, each expansion takes O(n) time.
Space Complexity: O(1) auxiliary space – Only a constant number of variables are used, excluding the space required to store the resulting substring.
Key Takeaway

The Expand Around Center technique is an intuitive and efficient way to solve palindrome-related problems.

By checking both odd-length and even-length centers, we can find the longest palindromic substring in O(n²) time without using additional data structures or dynamic programming.
